    "updated_at": "2016-12-22T18:40:44Z",
    "body": "For context (and to make this more concrete):\r\nThis is relevant to https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-archive/2016Dec/0001.html\r\n\r\nThere, we have a an Acid3 testcase with `<img src=\"<data URI>\" alt=\"\">`, which the spec says [\"represents nothing\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#the-img-element:attr-img-src-5) until the image is \"available\". (And the image is considered \"unavailable\" until the UA has [\"decoded enough of the image to get the image dimensions\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#img-none)).\r\n\r\nAnd the testcase uses JS to query this `<img>` *immediately after it's been added to the DOM* to get its dimensions. In current Firefox versions, the image data is decoded asynchronously on a helper thread, so the image is in this \"unavailable\" (and hence \"represents nothing\") state when the JS queries it for its size.  (But arguably the UA **does** \"expect this to change\", since we know that it's currently being decoded on a helper thread.)\r\n\r\nSo the question is: when JS queries for its size, should we apply the spec text that I quoted above and treat it as an empty inline element?  (in which case CSS \"height\" and \"width\" do not apply)  For now, that's what Firefox does -- but that breaks the expectations of this acid3 test.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
